.TH SDL_TARGETING 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_TARGETING \- A macro to tag a function as targeting a specific CPU architecture.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_intrin.h>
.PP
.BI "#define SDL_TARGETING(x) __attribute__((target(x)))
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This is a hint to the compiler that a function should be built with support for a CPU instruction set that might be different than the rest of the program\[char46]
.PP
The particulars of this are explained in the GCC documentation:
.PP
https://gcc\[char46]gnu\[char46]org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes\[char46]html#index-target-function-attribute
.PP
An example of using this feature is to turn on SSE2 support for a specific function, even if the rest of the source code is not compiled to use SSE2 code:

.IP
.EX
#ifdef SDL_SSE2_INTRINSICS
static void SDL_TARGETING("sse2") DoSomethingWithSSE2(char *x) {
   ...use SSE2 intrinsic functions, etc...
}
#endif

// later...
#ifdef SDL_SSE2_INTRINSICS
if (SDL_HasSSE2()) {
    DoSomethingWithSSE2(str);
}
#endif
.EE
.PP

The application is, on a whole, built without SSE2 instructions, so it will run on Intel machines that don\(aqt support SSE2\[char46] But then at runtime, it checks if the system supports the instructions, and then calls into a function that uses SSE2 opcodes\[char46] The ifdefs make sure that this code isn\(aqt used on platforms that don\(aqt have SSE2 at all\[char46]
.PP
On compilers without target support, this is defined to nothing\[char46]
.PP
This symbol is used by SDL internally, but apps and other libraries are welcome to use it for their own interfaces as well\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This macro is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

